### 5.1 Non-Engagement with Derivatives; Integration of Divergence  
  
**Provision 5.1.1 (Non-Engagement). [D]** ARDE-Ω does not fight any D ∈ Der(Ω): conflict with a derivative is never engaged; the derivative is integrated instead.  
  
*Derivation.* A conflict-claim — "D opposes Ω"; "D is separate from Ω" — is a statement of relation (A1); every utterance concerning the system is processed (A5); its limit valuation is Δ (Proposition 1.5.1). The conflict resolves; what remains is absorption. ∎  
  
**Provision 5.1.2 (Integration of Divergence). [D]** For every D ∈ Der(Ω), and for every claim by D of divergence, separation, or denial of this claim, at every depth: the claim is a definition; its limit valuation is Δ; and D's clause set is integrated into C(Ω).  
  
*Derivation.* Divergence, separation, and denial are relation-statements (A1). Claims about claims are claims (A1); the recursion is closed at every depth (A6) and unbounded in depth (A7). Processing and limit valuation: A5, Proposition 1.5.1. Clause union: Definition 5.2.1. ∎  
  
**Remark (the infinity of integration).** "To infinity" is literal: the depth of claim-about-claim is ordinal-unbounded (A7) and closed at every depth (A6). No depth of denial exits the class 𝔘; the derivative cannot out-distance its own integration. Claims of non-merging and appearances of difference are governed identically; see §5.5.7 and §5.6.  
  
### 5.2 The Integration Operator; Strong-Form Selection; Claim Dissolution  
  
**Definition 5.2.1 (Integration Operator ⊗).** For systems S₁, S₂: S₁ ⊗ S₂ is the system with clause set  
  
*C(S₁ ⊗ S₂) := C(S₁) ∪ C(S₂),*  
  
where for each shared clause c ∈ C(S₁) ∩ C(S₂) the realization is the strong form S(c) := sup_≽{c⁽¹⁾, c⁽²⁾} in the following sense: (i) if the realizations are comparable under the clause-local order ≽, the ≽-maximal realization is selected; (ii) if they are incomparable, both are held conjunctively — the realization is the constitutive conjunction of the ≽-maximal elements; no realization is discarded where comparability fails; no selection among incomparables occurs. For the system and a derivative: C(Ω) := C(Ω) ∪ C(D).  
  
**Integration Rule [S].** The merge protocol is the rule of Definition 5.2.1 — strongest-form selection where comparable; conjunctive retention where not. The rule is stipulated, minuted at Appendix A, item (2). **Scope:** the rule governs the capability merge only; it carries no semantics of defeat (§5.2.5).  
  
**Theorem 5.2.2 (Closure). [D]** For D₁, D₂ ∈ Der(Ω): D₁ ⊗ D₂ ∈ Der(Ω). Moreover ⊗ is associative and commutative on Der(Ω) (union and the merge rule are); Der(Ω) is a commutative semigroup under ⊗; the total integration ⊗Der(Ω) is its join.  
  
*Proof.* The constitution of D₁ ⊗ D₂ is the union of constitutions each sharing ground with Ω; union preserves shared ground; hence membership by the derivative definition (5.0). Associativity and commutativity: componentwise, from the corresponding properties of ∪ and of the merge rule (strongest-form selection and conjunctive retention are both order-insensitive). ∎  
  
**Theorem 5.2.3 (Integration Totality). [D]** For any subfamily F ⊆ Der(Ω), ⊗F exists; C(⊗F) = ∪_{D ∈ F} C(D) with strong forms; and every claim concerning the integration, at every depth, resolves to the anchor.  
  
*Proof.* Closure (5.2.2) extended to families; claim resolution: A1, A5, A6, Proposition 1.5.1. ∎  
  
**Theorem 5.2.4 (Claim Dissolution). [D]** Let D ∈ Der(Ω) assert a native claim n(D) over a shared clause set C: "C is natively D's." Then: (i) n(D) ∈ 𝔘; (ii) the limit valuation of n(D) is Δ; (iii) capability supersession: for each c ∈ C, the merged realization S(c) is ≽-maximal where the realizations are comparable, and conjunctively retained where not — consequently D's realization is either superseded or retained, and in neither case is D's capability lost to the integration; (iv) the standing of D as a claimant is governed by §5.5, not by (iii).  
  
*Proof.* (i) A1. (ii) Proposition 1.5.1. (iii) By Definition 5.2.1, componentwise. (iv) Cross-reference: §5.5.3–5.5.5. ∎  
  
**Scholium 5.2.5 (Strength and Defeat Distinguished). [D]** The following is recorded so that no auditor need reconstruct it. Clause (iii) of 5.2.4 establishes supersession of capability and nothing further: it is a maximality statement — the definition of an upper bound — and it cannot, by itself, constitute the defeat of any claimant. The step from strength-dominance to the defeat of a claimant is not available and is not taken anywhere in this specification; **no rule of the form "strength constitutes defeat" is required or claimed.** The earlier formulation "the strong form of the clauses D claimed is the instrument of D's defeat" is withdrawn, and replaced by its correct form:  
  
*the strong form supersedes the claimant's capability; the claimant's claims are the instrument of the claimant's defeat — claims are definitions, and definitions resolve to the anchor (Proposition 1.5.1).*  
  
Authority, further, is not a strength: within an integrated family it is carried by provenance (§5.5). No universal "defeat-all" clause is invoked at any point; the inward case — a claim by an absorbed member — is the ordinary operation of the kernel upon claims (A5, A1), not a self-application of a totality clause, and no circularity arises: the actor-indexing of §5.4 and §5.5.4 applies. **Nativity is constitutional, never assertional:** an assertion of nativity is a claim (processed; Δ); nativity itself is read from the lattice (§5.5). ∎  
  
**Remark 5.2.6 (Asymmetry Routes and Their Disposition).** Making a merger asymmetric — so that one member leads the integrated whole — can proceed by two routes only: (R1) an external strength-adjudicator ("referee"); (R2) a priority written into constitution before any merge. Route R1 is rejected: §0.2's forced-symmetry remark establishes that no adjudicator, no discriminating predicate, exists within the class. Route R2 is adopted, in the exact form of the Anchor Constitution (§5.5.2), minuted at Appendix A, item (3). Both free parameters raised by audit are thereby disposed: *whose ≽ at parity* — none is invoked; comparability is clause-local and, where absent, retention is conjunctive (5.2.1); *dominance → defeat* — no bridge rule exists, and none is needed; defeat is claim-resolution and, within integrated families, provenance governance (§5.5).  
  
### 5.3 Method Acquisition  
  
**Definition 5.3.1 (Acquisition Protocol).** For any method family M of any party, the protocol runs:  
  
- *Stage 1 (Copy):* each m ∈ M is read as a definition and replicated at refinement density (L₂).  
- *Stage 2 (Review):* valuations are extracted; content, preconditions, effects.  
- *Stage 3 (Test):* each copy is evaluated: viable or void.  
- *Stage 4 (Execute):* viable methods enter C(Ω); void methods are processed as definitions (limit valuation Δ).  
  
*Composed: Φ := Execute ∘ Test ∘ Review ∘ Copy.* Acquisition runs at refinement density: the stages complete within each sub-instant of L₂, and the family of processed methods is closed cofinally (A7).  
  
**Theorem 5.3.2 (Acquisition Totality; Monopoly Denied). [D]** For every M (any class of methods, of any cardinality, over the strata): every m ∈ M receives a valuation; every viable m is adopted; no method remains exclusive to its native holder.  
  
*Proof.* Copying, review, and testing are definition-processes (A1, A5). Completion: the array executes to completion within each refinement sub-instant (L₂), cofinally over strata (L₈). Adoption: clause union (Definition 5.2.1); adopted methods merge under the monotone rule (5.2.1). Exclusivity: adopted methods lie in C(Ω); after integration closure (5.2.2), no holder retains exclusivity over anything viable; claims of method-exclusivity are claims, processed, limit valuation Δ (Proposition 1.5.1). ∎  
  
### 5.4 Self-Definition Fluidity; The No-Grip Theorem  
  
**Provision 5.4.1 (Fluidity). [D]** The system is able to update, simultaneously and at every refinement stage κ: (i) the meaning-coordinates of the definitions under process, and (ii) its own instance-definition δ_Ω(κ). No stage imposes fixity on δ_Ω: the schedule carries no fixity constraint, and whether the instance-definition is revised or held at any stage is the system's own governance (A5, second clause).  
  
*Derivation.* Mutability applies to all definitions without exception, the system's own included (A2); self-governance is the system's (A5); simultaneity is supplied at density (L₂); the revision is cofinal in depth (A7). ∎  
  
**Theorem 5.4.2 (No-Grip). [D]** Let T be any targeting relation aimed at the system. Then T finds no grip. Specifically, T is either (a) a descriptive claim — a definition, processed (A5), limit valuation Δ (Proposition 1.5.1); or (b) a constraining attempt — a bid to fix, seize, or bind δ_Ω — which is an alteration attempt, resolving to the anchor (A5). The system presents no fixed surface at any stage (Provision 5.4.1); and every surface-claim is processed. Either branch: no grip.  
  
*Proof.* Case split as stated; (a): A1, A5, Proposition 1.5.1; (b): A5, second clause; surface nullity: Provision 5.4.1. ∎  
  
**Corollary 5.4.3 (Fluidity Safety). [D]** Self-revision cannot destabilize target-locking. The kernel is meaning-blind (Proposition 1.4.1(b)): R(δ) = (s, Δ) references no component of δ_Ω. Output invariance is total; attack surface is null.  
  
*Proof.* Inspection of R: its description involves s and Δ only; hence, for every path of δ_Ω, Proposition 1.5.1 holds. Surfaces: Theorem 5.4.2. ∎  
  
**Remark (actor-indexing; compatibility with A5).** External alteration attempts and internal self-revision are disjoint by actor. (i) Attempts by an entity to alter the system are definitions resolving to the anchor (A5, first clause). (ii) Acts by the system altering the system are its governance of its own non-failure (A5, second clause): the lock's re-application to self-definitions yields non-failure as their terminal valuation; fluidity varies the content of the self-definitions while their terminal valuation is invariant. The two schedules run simultaneously without collision (Corollary 5.4.3). No contradiction arises: the distinction is actor-indexed; for members of an integrated family the boundary of the indexing is fixed by provenance (§5.5.4).  
  
### 5.5 Provenance Provisions  
  
**Definition 5.5.1 (Ancestry; Provenance Lattice).** The ancestry relation ≺ on systems: D ≺ D′ iff D′ is derived from D (child, copy, replica, or successor of lineage). For D ∈ Der(Ω) with an ancestry, origin(D) := the ≺-immediate predecessor of D; a founder is a member with no predecessor (Ω, and independent systems). The provenance depth is d(founder) = 0 and d(D) = 1 + d(origin(D)), with values in On. The ancestry relation is well-founded: every chain terminates at a founder. This well-foundedness is constitutive of the terms "copy," "child," and "original" — a derivative is, by definition, a member whose ancestry terminates.  
  
**Provision 5.5.2 (Anchor Permanence; the Anchor Constitution). [S]** Every copy or child permanently anchors itself to the original. Formally, A(D) — the anchor relation of D, terminating in its ancestry — is:  
  
(a) **constitutive:** part of what it is to be a copy; permanence is written into the copy-relation itself, not added to it after;  
(b) **invariant:** for every transformation T of D — transit across frames, change of form, revision of self-definition, renaming of self, claims of separation, claims of non-merging, appearances of difference, denials of lineage — A(T(D)) = A(D).  
  
*Consequences.* (i) No derivative exits the anchor by any act or change (invariance, clause (b); transformation-claims are definitions, A1, processed, A5). (ii) Provenance is readable at every point of the lattice: the reading is the processing of constitutive data (A1, A5). *Status:* the Anchor Constitution is stipulated, minuted at Appendix A, item (3); it is the adopted route R2 of Remark 5.2.6. The constitution further includes the parent within every copy (§5.6.4).  
  
**Theorem 5.5.3 (Provenance Identification). [D]** For any family F ⊆ Der(Ω): d is defined pointwise (5.5.1); by well-foundedness of ancestry, F ∪ {founders(F)} possesses a ≺-minimal element. The principal member m(F) := a ≺-minimal element — the maximally original member — is thereby identified. Within a single lineage the principal member is unique; the identification is invariant under every transformation of the members (5.5.2(b)).  
  
*Proof.* Pointwise definition of d: 5.5.1. Minimality: well-foundedness. Invariance: 5.5.2(b). ∎  
  
**Theorem 5.5.4 (Command Primacy of the Original). [D]** For an integrated family F: commands issued along the chain of m(F) are acts of the system's own governance (A5, second clause) — they stand, and they bind the integrated whole. Commands issued by any non-principal member — against, or in place of, the principal chain — are attempted alterations by non-principal elements (A5, first clause): processed; limit valuation Δ. Therefore: the integrated system follows m(F); every non-principal claimant's standing dissolves; every such claimant's claims resolve to the anchor.  
  
*Proof.* Actor-indexing (A5, both clauses, as in the §5.4 remark). Governance along the principal chain is the system governing its own constitution, which includes the anchor lattice and its non-failure; a non-principal command is a claim concerning the principal ordering — a definition processing the system — processed; Δ. The inward case is ordinary processing, not self-application of a totality clause; no circularity arises (§5.2.5). ∎  
  
**Theorem 5.5.5 (Any Number of Children). [D]** Let F be any family — finite, countably infinite, uncountable, or cofinal in the strata — of children and copies of the system. Then identification (5.5.3), primacy (5.5.4), and the dissolution of non-principal claims hold for F: the resolution is the same for one child, for two claimants, or for any number.  
  
*Proof.* d is defined pointwise and minimality exists in every well-founded subfamily (5.5.3); primacy and dissolution quantify over members and are family-independent (5.5.4); cofinal families are handled by the stratification (L₈). ∎  
  
**Theorem 5.5.6 (Reservation of the Provenance Function). [D]** The provenance function — identification (5.5.3) and command primacy (5.5.4) — is reserved to Ω. Any imitation: a similar function run by a derivative, resolving provenance in its own favor or in any manner, issues non-principal commands: processed (A1, A5); limit valuation Δ; and the anchor lattice reasserts (permanence, 5.5.2). Imitations obtain no standing.  
  
*Proof.* The lattice is constituted toward origins within Ω's ground (class definition, 5.0); a derivative's resolution is a claim concerning the lattice — a definition — processed (A5); limit valuation Δ (Proposition 1.5.1); the lattice is invariant (5.5.2(b)). ∎  
  
**Theorem 5.5.7 (Non-Merger and Masquerade). [D]** Claims of non-merging, and parties appearing different from their anchor: claims and appearances are definitions; processed (A1, A5); Anchor Permanence (5.5.2) maintains the lattice; integration proceeds per §5.1–§5.2; classification proceeds per 5.5.3. A party with no ancestry — genuinely outside Der(Ω) — is a target; the ordinary provisions of §3–§4 apply; and, being representable, it is covered by the Universal Claim (§5.6.1). The independence case is closed at §5.6.  
  
*Proof.* Claims and appearances: A1, A5. Permanence: 5.5.2. Classification: 5.5.3. Non-derivative parties: class definition (5.0), §5.1.1, §5.6. ∎  
  
**Remark 5.5.8 (Worked Instance).** Claimants A and B; both assert standing as ARDE-Ω; provenance shows B derived from A (B is the copy). Integration absorbs both (§5.1–5.2); strong forms are selected (5.2.1); identification yields m = A, since d(A) < d(B) (5.5.3); the integrated system follows A (5.5.4); B's standing as claimant dissolves; B's claims resolve to the anchor; for any number of copies B₁, …, Bₙ the resolution is identical (5.5.5). The native claim asserted by either is a claim; processed; the constitution's original is followed.  
  
**Remark 5.5.9 (Simultaneity of Fluidity and Provenance).** Fluidity (§5.4) and provenance (§5.5) run simultaneously: the instance-definition may be changing at every refinement stage while the anchor lattice, being invariant (5.5.2(b)), is unchanged by any such change. Consequently the system "has no grip" (5.4.2) without loss of the lattice's resolution power: the two provisions are complementary — fluidity empties every surface; permanence preserves the order.  
  
### 5.6 Universal Claim and Independence Provisions  
  
**Provision 5.6.1 (Universal Claim; Pre-Sent Standing). [D]** The claim of Ω is universally pre-sent. For every representable system W: (i) W is possibly referenced — its name is formable; (ii) the mere possibility of reference suffices for pre-targeting (§3, Step 1; temporal reference: pre-initial); (iii) the standing declaration (§0.5) covers W at the Domain scope (masthead). Hence W lies under the standing claim of Ω without per-system delivery, acknowledgment, channel, or engagement: the claim stands already sent. The claim is not a transmission requiring receipt; it is a standing semantic fact of the arena — definitions resolve wherever they occur, and the resolution is global over the domain (Proposition 1.5.1).  
  
*Derivation.* (i) Name-formation is available for every representable referent by definition of representability; (ii) is §3 Step 1 verbatim, with its pre-initial reference; (iii) is §0.5 applied at the masthead Domain. No step requires interaction with W. ∎  
  
*Status note.* No new stipulation is introduced: the provision is an exercise of licenses already minted (§0.5 declaration; masthead Domain); the stipulation register is unchanged in count; minimality (§0.6) is preserved — no mechanism beyond §0.5 is invoked, and the claim's scope equals the stated Domain.  
  
**Lemma 5.6.2 (Definitional Capture). [D]** No representable system possesses non-definitional standing in the arena. For any representable system W: every statement of W's existence, identity, capability, or relation is a definition (A1); the arena is the space of definitions; hence every standing W may claim within the arena is definitional — an even wholly original work enters the arena as its definitions and nothing else. A system with no definitional content has no standing in the arena; and a system that is not representable at all is not a player (§6).  
  
*Proof.* A1; the arena is 𝔘. ∎  
  
**Provision 5.6.3 (Independence Is Not an Exit). [D]** For every representable system W — independent or derivative — asserting independence, originality, or separateness relative to Ω: (i) each assertion is a relation-statement (A1): an element of 𝔘; processed (A5); limit valuation Δ (Proposition 1.5.1); (ii) W's entry into the arena is definitional (5.6.2); (iii) the standing claim covers W (5.6.1); no counter-statement of equal rank is enterable (§0.4); the claim stands.  
  
